# Exercise: A Tiny CLI Summary
#
# Time to put count + at + bounds-check together into something
# that looks like a real CLI helper. The goal: a word that
# inspects the arguments and returns a one-line summary string.
#
# Behaviour:
#
#   no user args        → "no arguments provided"
#   one user arg "foo"  → "got: foo"
#   two or more args    → "got <N> arguments starting with <first>"
#
# All three branches are reachable on a real command line. Under
# `seqc test`, only the first branch fires (no user args), so the
# test asserts that exact result.
#
# Your task: Write `summary ( -- String )`.
#
# Useful pieces:
#   args.count                                 the total
#   args.count 1 i.-                           number of *user* args
#   1 args.at                                  first user arg (if any)
#   string.concat                              ( a b -- ab )
#   int->string                                ( Int -- String )
#
# ## To see all three branches on a real command line
#
# The seqlings test only fires the no-args branch. To exercise the
# others once you've solved it:
#
#     ─── try-summary.seq ───────────────────────────
#     include "exercises/21-args/04-combine"
#
#     : main ( -- Int )
#         summary io.write-line
#         0
#     ;
#     ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     $ seqc build try-summary.seq -o try-summary
#     $ ./try-summary                  → no arguments provided
#     $ ./try-summary hello            → got: hello
#     $ ./try-summary hello world      → got 2 arguments starting with hello
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: summary ( -- String )
    # Branch on (args.count - 1):
    #   0 → "no arguments provided"
    #   1 → "got: " + (1 args.at)
    #   _ → "got " + count + " arguments starting with " + (1 args.at)
    "TODO"
;

: test-no-args ( -- )
    summary
    # Do not edit below this line
    "no arguments provided" string.equal? test.assert
;
